/**
 * A bf16 or an f16 writes the shortest digits that read back as the same element of its own type
 * (design principles 7.7, "reduced-precision-text"): `0.1 as bf16` prints `0.1`, where the f32 text
 * of the value it widens to would print `0.100097656`.
 *
 * The digits are found in camel/utils/number_text.h, `shortestHalfDigits`, and they must survive the
 * call that found them: the search builds each candidate in a buffer of its own and the text is read
 * after that call returns, so a candidate handed back as a view of the buffer is a view of a dead
 * frame -- on one build it read as empty for `0.1 as bf16`, and an empty digit string lays out a
 * text with no digits in it. This case pins the digits themselves, for both reduced types, on the
 * values the search trims and the values it does not.
 *
 * This is not the regression test of that read: whether a frame is reused is up to the build, so a
 * compiler that leaves the buffer alone prints these values correctly with the view still pointing
 * at it. What is pinned here is the text the language promises, on both platforms; the read of the
 * dead frame has its one reading on the Windows leg, where a check's report stopped inside a bf16
 * counterexample (2026-11-02, `rules.library_verdicts_are_exactly_these`).
 */

func main(): int sync {
    println(bf16([0.1, -0.1, 1.0, -1.0, 100.0, -100.0]))
    println(f16([0.1, -0.1, 1.0, -1.0, 100.0, -100.0]))
    return 0
}